,8,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9],[9,9,9,9,9,8,8,8,8,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,2,2,2,2],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,6,6,6,2],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,2,2,2,2,2,6,6,6,2],[8,8,8,8,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,3,3,3,2,2,6,6,6,2],[8,3,3,3,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,3,3,3,2,2,2,2,2,2],[8,3,3,3,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,3,3,3,2,9,9,9,9,9],[8,3,3,3,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,2,2,2,2,9,9,9,9,9],[8,8,8,8,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"Every object in these grids is the same kind of thing: a 5×5 square made of a one-colour frame around a 3×3 core of a different colour. The frames are only ever colour 8 or colour 2. In all three train pairs the squares keep their rows but move sideways:\n\n- **8-framed squares slide left** until they hit the left edge.\n- **2-framed squares slide right** until they hit the right edge.\n\nThe movement works like gravity, with squares stacking against one another. In pair 1, the 8-square at rows 3–7 cannot reach x=0, because the 8-square at rows 1–5 is already against the wall and their rows overlap. It stops directly beside it, at x=5–9.\n\nThe order of the moves matters. The square nearest its wall moves first. In pair 1, the 2-square at rows 16–20 starts at x=5, which is closer to the right edge than the rows 13–17 square at x=0. So it reaches x=17–21 first. The rows 13–17 square then lands beside it at x=12–16. The rows 7–11 square stops against the rows 9–13 square at x=12–16 in the same way.\n\nSquares only block squares of their own frame colour. Each colour group falls on its own and does not pass through the other group's lanes.\n\n**Finding the squares.** I cannot use connected components. Same-coloured frames often touch: in pair 1 two 2-frames merge into a single object of size 32. Instead I scan for every 5×5 window that meets three conditions:\n- its border is a single non-background colour;\n- its interior is a single colour;\n- that interior colour differs from both the border and the background.\n\nAny window I accept marks its cells as used, so the same square cannot be found twice.\n\n**Moving them.**\n- For the left group, I sort the squares by x ascending. Each one goes to the largest right edge among already-placed, row-overlapping squares, or to 0 if there is none.\n- For the right group, I mirror this: sort by x descending, and place each square at W−5 or just left of the nearest blocker.\n\n**The test input.** Background 9. Four 8-squares slide left:\n- the square at rows 7–11 reaches the wall;\n- the rows 3–7 square stacks against it, since rows 3–7 overlap rows 7–11;\n- the rows 8–12 square also stacks against it;\n- the rows 16–20 square reaches the wall.\n\nFour 2-squares slide right and stack in the same way.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    used = np.zeros_like(g, dtype=bool)\n    squares = []\n    for y in range(H - 4):\n        for x in range(W - 4):\n            if used[y:y+5, x:x+5].any():\n                continue\n            blk = g[y:y+5, x:x+5]\n            border = np.concatenate([blk[0], blk[4], blk[1:4, 0], blk[1:4, 4]])\n            inner = blk[1:4, 1:4]\n            c = border[0]\n            ic = inner[0, 0]\n            if c == bg or not (border == c).all() or not (inner == ic).all():\n                continue\n            if ic == c or ic == bg:\n                continue\n            used[y:y+5, x:x+5] = True\n            squares.append({\"y\": y, \"x\": x, \"frame\": int(c), \"blk\": blk.copy()})\n\n    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Clear the canvas to background; squares will be re-placed by sliding.\")\n\n    direction = {8: \"left\", 2: \"right\"}\n    for col, d in direction.items():\n        grp = [s for s in squares if s[\"frame\"] == col]\n        if not grp:\n            continue\n        placed = []\n        grp.sort(key=lambda s: s[\"x\"], reverse=(d == \"right\"))\n        for s in grp:\n            y = s[\"y\"]\n            blockers = [p for p in placed if abs(p[0] - y) < 5]\n            if d == \"left\":\n                nx = max([p[1] + 5 for p in blockers], default=0)\n            else:\n                nx = min([p[1] - 5 for p in blockers], default=W - 5)\n            placed.append((y, nx))\n            out[y:y+5, nx:nx+5] = s[\"blk\"]\n        emit(out.copy(), f\"Slide every square framed in colour {col} to the {d} edge, stacking against squares already there.\")\n\n    # any square of another frame colour stays put\n    for s in squares:\n        if s[\"frame\"] not in direction:\n            out[s[\"y\"]:s[\"y\"]+5, s[\"x\"]:s[\"x\"]+5] = s[\"blk\"]\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Final arrangement.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each one goes to the largest right edge among already-placed, row-overlapping squares, or to 0 if there is none."}],"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- For the right group, I mirror this: sort by x descending, and place each square at W−5 or just left of the nearest blocker."}],"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **8-framed squares slide left** until they hit the left edge."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **2-framed squares slide right** until they hit the right edge."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Four 8-squares slide left:"}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- its interior is a single colour;"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- that interior colour differs from both the border and the background."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The movement works like gravity, with squares stacking against one another."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each colour group falls on its own and does not pass through the other group's lanes."}],"order":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- For the left group, I sort the squares by x ascending."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- For the right group, I mirror this: sort by x descending, and place each square at W−5 or just left of the nearest blocker."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"s 3–7 cannot reach x=0, because the 8-square at rows 1–5 is already against the wall and their rows overlap."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each one goes to the largest right edge among already-placed, row-overlapping squares, or to 0 if there is none."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- the rows 3–7 square stacks against it, since rows 3–7 overlap rows 7–11;"}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"9d0a49cce580cbb930ba85f7ca168cec996f9f2465e2715e4c26f369e253daaa","task_sha256":"7eaa63df0ce22b36bd83de6dd7edb7067dd469231eff348c1a387f0afca1c36a","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
